---
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Last Ride Registry — every US cyclist killed by a driver, 2010–2024</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="An open registry of the 13,050 people killed while riding bicycles on US roads since 2010. Open data, verified accountability tracking, built to be used."
    />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Last Ride Registry" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="13,050 rides never made it home. An open registry of every US cyclist killed by a driver since 2010."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <!-- ============ scroll experience ============ -->
    <div id="experience">
      <canvas id="ride-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

      <div id="hero">
        <h1>Last Ride<br /><em>Registry</em></h1>
        <p class="sub">Every cyclist killed by a driver on a US road · 2010–2024</p>
        <p class="scroll-cue">scroll</p>
      </div>

      <div id="hud" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="hud-year">2010</div>
        <div class="hud-count">0</div>
        <div class="hud-label">riders killed</div>
      </div>

      <section class="scene" data-scene="one" aria-label="One ride">
        <div class="scene-card">
          <p class="kicker">One ride</p>
          <p>
            A ride is a line — from somewhere, toward somewhere. On the evening
            of&#32;<strong>January&nbsp;2,&nbsp;2010</strong>, on an unlit road in Worcester County,
            Maryland, a driver struck and killed a&#32;<strong>23-year-old man</strong> on a bicycle.
          </p>
          <p>His line stopped there. It was the second day of the decade.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="scene scene--tall" data-scene="replay" aria-label="Fifteen years replayed">
        <div class="scene-card">
          <p class="kicker">Fifteen years</p>
          <p>
            Keep scrolling. Every light is a person on a bicycle, in the place where a driver
            killed them —&#32;<strong>replayed in the order it happened</strong>, 2010 through 2024.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="scene" data-scene="toll" aria-label="The toll">
        <div class="scene-card">
          <p class="kicker">The toll</p>
          <p>
            <span class="stat">13,050 people</span> were killed riding bicycles on American roads
            in fifteen years. Annual deaths rose&#32;<strong>88%</strong> — from 623 in 2010 to a
            record 1,173 in 2023. The preliminary 2024 count dips to 1,103; NHTSA will revise it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nearly half were killed in the dark. More were killed by SUVs and pickups than ever
            before. The median victim was not a child on a cul-de-sac; it was an adult in their
            fifties, riding a road they likely knew well.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="scene" data-scene="fled" aria-label="The drivers who fled">
        <div class="scene-card">
          <p class="kicker">The drivers</p>
          <p>
            The red lights are hit-and-runs. In 2010,&#32;<span class="stat stat--red">15%</span> of
            drivers who killed a cyclist fled the scene. By 2023 it was&#32;<span
              class="stat stat--red">23%</span> — nearly one in four.
          </p>
          <p>
            And after the crash?&#32;<strong>No agency in America tracks what happens to the
            drivers.</strong> Whether they were charged, convicted, or simply drove home is not
            recorded anywhere — so we are building the registry that records it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="scene" data-scene="handoff" aria-label="Explore">
        <div class="scene-card">
          <p class="kicker">The registry</p>
          <p>
            Every one of these deaths is in the open dataset below — mapped, documented, and free
            for anyone to use. Explore the map. Take the data. Hold someone accountable.
          </p>
          <div class="cta-row">
            <a class="btn btn--amber" href="#registry">Explore the map</a>
            <a class="btn" href="#data">Download the data</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ registry map ============ -->
    <section id="registry" aria-label="Registry map">
      <div class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <p class="kicker">The registry</p>
        <h2>12,985 places where a ride ended</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Each white bike marks one person, at the location recorded by the responding agency.
          Zoom in to your town. Click a marker for what is known — and what is not yet known.
        </p>
        <p class="mono" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
          65 deaths lack precise coordinates; they are in the CSV, not on the map.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="map-filters">
        <div class="year-filter">
          <span>Years</span>
          <div class="range-pair">
            <input type="range" id="year-min" min="2010" max="2024" value="2010" aria-label="First year" />
            <input type="range" id="year-max" min="2010" max="2024" value="2024" aria-label="Last year" />
          </div>
          <span id="year-readout">2010–2024</span>
        </div>
        <label><input type="checkbox" id="f-hitrun" class="red" /> hit-and-run only</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox" id="f-dark" /> after dark only</label>
        <span id="map-count" style="color: var(--amber);"></span>
      </div>
      <div id="map-wrap">
        <div id="registry-map"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ recent, curated ============ -->
    <section id="recent" aria-label="Recent deaths">
      <div class="section">
        <p class="kicker">Too recent for the federal data</p>
        <h2>The registry doesn't wait eighteen months</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Federal crash data lags a year or more. The deaths below are curated by hand from
          published news reports — named, cited, and updated as the legal process moves. Our
          rules for what gets published, and how to request a correction, are in the
          open:&#32;<a href="https://github.com/f-mac/lastrideregistry/blob/main/EDITORIAL.md">EDITORIAL.md</a>.
        </p>
        <div id="recent-cases"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <!-- ============ the pattern ============ -->
    <section id="pattern" aria-label="The pattern">
      <div class="section">
        <p class="kicker">The pattern</p>
        <h2>This is not an accident. It is a trend.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Fifteen years of federal crash data, one row per person. The numbers below reconcile
          with NHTSA's published totals to within a fraction of a percent — check our math, it's
          all downloadable.
        </p>
        <div class="chart-grid" id="charts"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <!-- ============ open data ============ -->
    <section id="data" aria-label="Open data">
      <div class="section">
        <p class="kicker">Open data</p>
        <h2>Take it. Build on it. That's the point.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The White Line, city dashboards, and federal reports let you look. This registry lets
          you <em>use</em>: one clean CSV, one row per person killed, versioned in the open.
        </p>
        <div class="data-cols">
          <div>
            <h3>The dataset</h3>
            <p>
              Every pedalcyclist fatality in NHTSA's Fatality Analysis Reporting System,
              2010–2024, cleaned and joined: date, location (99.5% geocoded), age, sex, light
              condition, striking vehicle class, and crash-level hit-and-run, impaired-driver and
              speeding flags.
            </p>
            <div class="file-row">
              <a href="/data/cyclist_fatalities.csv" download>cyclist_fatalities.csv</a>
              <span class="size">13,050 rows · 1.4 MB · CC BY 4.0</span>
            </div>
            <div class="file-row">
              <a href="/data/DATA_DICTIONARY.md" download>DATA_DICTIONARY.md</a>
              <span class="size">column definitions &amp; caveats</span>
            </div>
            <div class="file-row">
              <a href="/data/aggregates.json">aggregates.json</a>
              <span class="size">pre-computed chart data · JSON</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Methodology</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Source: NHTSA FARS annual files (public domain), person type 6–7, fatal injury.</li>
              <li>Validated: yearly totals match NHTSA published counts (2010: exact; 2023: 1,173 exact).</li>
              <li>Hit-and-run, impairment and speeding are crash-level flags — "any vehicle in the crash," NHTSA's own convention.</li>
              <li>2024 is NHTSA's Annual Report File and will be revised; we re-release when FARS finalizes.</li>
              <li>Pipeline code is open source — every transform is inspectable and re-runnable.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>License</h3>
            <p>
              Data: <strong>CC BY 4.0</strong> — use it for anything, credit "Last Ride
              Registry." Code: <strong>MIT</strong>. Upstream FARS data is US-government public
              domain. Cite it, fork it, print it, take it to a city council meeting.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <!-- ============ what's next ============ -->
    <section id="next" aria-label="What comes next">
      <div class="section">
        <p class="kicker">What comes next</p>
        <h2>The question no dataset answers: what happened to the driver?</h2>
        <div class="roadmap">
          <div class="step now">
            <span class="tag">Now — v1</span>
            <h3>The open registry</h3>
            <p>Every death since 2010, mapped and downloadable. You are looking at it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="tag">Next — v2</span>
            <h3>Verified accountability</h3>
            <p>
              For every 2023 hit-and-run death — roughly 275 cases, old enough that prosecutions
              have resolved — we will trace the outcome through court records: charged or not,
              convicted or not, sentence or nothing. Not scraped headlines. Records. Published
              as open data, case by case, with citations.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="tag">Then — v3</span>
            <h3>Every year, every case</h3>
            <p>
              Expand cohort by cohort until "was the driver charged?" has a public answer for
              every cyclist killed in America — and a correction channel so families and local
              advocates can keep it honest.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <div class="section">
        <p>
          LAST RIDE REGISTRY · lastrideregistry.com · Fatality data: NHTSA Fatality Analysis
          Reporting System (public domain). Site data © Last Ride Registry, CC BY 4.0. Code MIT.
          Basemap © <a href="https://carto.com/attributions">CARTO</a> ·
          © <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors.
          Built in memory of everyone whose ride ended here. Corrections and contributions
          welcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <div id="tooltip" role="tooltip"></div>

    <script>
      import '../scripts/landing.js';
      import '../scripts/registry-map.js';
      import '../scripts/charts.js';
      import '../scripts/recent.js';
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
